#
# PATTERN SUMMARY: Universally we over-select DCT32X16 (and to a lesser
# extent DCT16X32 and DCT32X32). Universally we under-select DCT16X16
# (the strongest signal, -6815 Y_delta total). The "missing DCT16X16"
# Y_delta IS the "extra DCT32X16" Y_delta: cjxl resolves at the 16x16-
# level (4 DCT16X16 covering 16 cells per 32x32) where we resolve at
# 32x16-level (2 DCT32X16 covering 16 cells per 32x32). Both fit the
# same coverage; the difference is token-count overhead.
#
# STRATEGY AGREEMENT (fill-the-plane %)
# =====================================
#
# cell                    agree_pct   bytes_pct_delta
# 1420710 e5 d5           90.33%     +3.67%
# 1420710 e5 d6           89.84%     +4.02%
# 1420710 e7 d5           90.92%     +3.36%
# 1531677 e5 d5           85.16%     +3.54%
# 1531677 e6 d5           83.59%     +3.04%
# 1531677 e8 d5           86.33%     +3.05%
# 1531677 e9 d5           86.33%     +3.64%
#
# Mean agreement: 87.50%   Mean byte overshoot: +3.47%
#
# Note: 1531677 e8 d5 == e9 d5 (md5sum identical) — butteraugli iters at
# e8 (2 iters) and e9 (4 iters) don't change strategy SELECTION on this
# cell. cjxl's e8 != e9 dumps (different) so libjxl IS refining at e9.
# Indicates: any refinement in our path isn't reaching strategy selection,
# AND our buttloop tweaks only the quant field (libjxl-parity behaviour).
#
# Note: 1531677 e5 d5 == e6 d5 (md5sum identical) — at e5 (no non-aligned
# eval) and e6 (non-aligned eval ON) we pick IDENTICAL strategies, while
# cjxl's e5 vs e6 dumps differ. Our `try_merge_16x16` / `try_merge_32x32`
# non-aligned passes are not picking up new merges on this cell.
#
# ROOT CAUSE HYPOTHESIS
# =====================
#
# RC1 (most likely): W44-29 + W44-96 tables maintain the libjxl ratio
#     dct16x32 = dct32x32 * (1.49/1.48) but with LOWER absolute values
#     (1.34→1.20 for dct32x32; 1.349→1.208 for dct16x32). The ratio is
#     preserved (DCT32X32 is 0.7% cheaper than 2× DCT32X16) — BUT the
#     ABSOLUTE entropy term is lifted relative to the LOSS term in
#     `entropy_mul * entropy + loss`. When entropy values are smaller
#     (the lift makes them smaller), the loss term becomes RELATIVELY
#     larger, and DCT32X16's loss is smaller than DCT32X32's loss
#     (smaller block → smaller IDCT roundtrip error on smooth content
#     because the smaller transform doesn't average 32×32 pixels).
#     This biases toward DCT32X16 SPLIT.
#
#     EVIDENCE: 1531677 (worst cell, +2425 DCT32X16 overspend) is the
#     image where the W44-96 sub-discriminator routes to variant Z
#     (the MOST aggressive lift). 1420710 e5 d=5 (also variant Z) has
#     +888 DCT32X16 overspend — large but smaller than 1531677.
#
# RC2 (also likely): The DCT32X16 vs DCT16X16 decision happens in
#     find_best_16x16 INSIDE find_best_32x32_transform. The `mul8x8`
#     external multiplier (libjxl `mul8x8 = 1.0 + (-0.4)/(d+1.4)`)
#     at d=5 = `1.0 - 0.4/6.4 = 0.9375`. Multi-block transforms get
#     `1.0` (no mul8x8). At d=6 = `1.0 - 0.4/7.4 = 0.946`. So mul8x8
#     gets STRONGER (more discount) at HIGHER d. This makes singles
#     RELATIVELY cheaper at higher d → drives DCT8/DCT16X8/DCT8X16
#     UP at the 8x8 step. cjxl reflects this (DCT16X8 cjxl 19, ours
#     0; DCT8 cjxl 39, ours 10).
#
#     EVIDENCE: We dramatically UNDER-fire DCT8, DCT16X8, DCT8X16 on
#     every single cell. cjxl picks DCT8 31-37 times per cell; we pick
#     9-10. The cost-model bias against single-block + 16x8 split
#     pushes us up to 32x16 split.
#
# RC3 (less likely): Could be the partial-merge sub-cost logic at libjxl
#     line 792-795:
#       costJxN = std::min(entropy_JXK_left, entropy[0][0] + entropy[1][0]) +
#                 std::min(entropy_JXK_right, entropy[0][1] + entropy[1][1]);
#     Our `find_best_32x32_transform_impl` at line 2570-2571 does:
#       cost_jxn = entropy_32x16_0.min(sub_left) + entropy_32x16_1.min(sub_right)
#     Where sub_left = quadrant_cost[0][0] + quadrant_cost[1][0]. This
#     matches. But the quadrant_cost values are built from cached
#     entropy_estimate values written by find_best_16x16 — and find_best_16x16
#     stores costs WITHOUT the parent mul8x8 baked in (because the parent
#     mul8x8 is applied only to single-block costs INSIDE find_best_16x16,
#     not at the storage level). Need to verify whether the cached costs
#     match libjxl exactly at the 16x16-stores-its-own-cost step.
#
# UNPORTED LIBJXL MECHANISM SEARCH
# =================================
#
# Searched libjxl `enc_ac_strategy.cc` for d=5-specific gates:
#
#   * line 587 `butteraugli_target < 5.0`: kFavor2X2 — we have it
#     (favor_2x2_weight in ac_strategy_search.rs:40)
#   * line 594 `butteraugli_target > 4.0`: kAvoidEntropyOfTransforms —
#     we have it (avoid_entropy_of_transforms_mul in ac_strategy_search.rs:60)
#   * line 936 `decoding_speed_tier < 4`: enable_32x32 — always true
#     at our default tier 0, libjxl-parity
#   * line 948 `decoding_speed_tier < 4`: enable DCT64 — guarded by
#     try_dct64 (effort>=7) at our end; W44-93 RULED OUT widening this
#
# Searched for d=5 in `enc_adaptive_quantization.cc`:
#   * no d=5-specific gates found (the AdjustQuantField runs at all d)
#
# Searched non_aligned_eval gate:
#   * libjxl `speed_tier >= kHare` (effort 5) early-returns BEFORE
#     non-aligned eval. Our `non_aligned_eval: effort >= 6` is LIBJXL-PARITY.
#   * At e8/e9 the non-aligned eval is enabled both sides, but doesn't
#     close the gap (per 1531677 e8 d5 == e9 d5 strategy md5 finding).
#
# Searched for "priority" array in ProcessRectACS:
#   * libjxl line 619-637 TryMergeAcs uses `priority[]` to PREVENT
#     overlapping merges (8x8-position priority gating). Our port replaces
#     this with `multi_block_crosses_*_boundary` + `can_evaluate_region_outer_only`
#     helpers. The TryMergeAcs fallthrough at line 1023-1026 only catches
#     edge cases of last row/column at odd boundaries. On 512×512 inputs
#     (our 7 cells) this fallthrough fires on no significant blocks.
#
# Searched for SetEntropyForTransform mismatch:
#   * libjxl line 798 stores `entropy_JXJ` (with entropy_mul_JXJ baked
#     in) for 16x16 winner. Our find_best_16x16 stores
#     `entropy_16x16 = mul16x16 * estimate_entropy(DCT16X16,...)`
#     where mul16x16 = 1.0 (pixel-domain) and entropy_mul_table.dct16x16
#     (= 1.27 in W44-29, 1.34 in libjxl) is baked in by estimate_entropy_with_mask.
#     LIBJXL parity in shape, OUR W44-29 lower value baked in.
#
# CONCLUSION: No single unported gate found. The pattern is a SECOND-ORDER
# effect of the W44-29 + W44-96 entropy_mul lifts — they preserve the
# inter-strategy ratio but shift the absolute entropy term down, making
# the loss term RELATIVELY larger, which on smooth-photo content biases
# toward smaller transforms (DCT32X16 over DCT32X32 or DCT16X16).
#
# RANKED W44-98 ATTACK CANDIDATES
# ================================
#
# Candidate A: Per-strategy entropy_mul lift on DCT32X16 only (lift dct16x32
#              ABOVE the dct32x32 baseline, breaking the W44-29 1.49/1.48
#              ratio) for the W44-96 variant Z sub-class. — MEDIUM-LOW RISK
#
#   Mechanism: in `EntropyMulTable::high_d_photo_smooth_suppressed_z()`
#   change `dct16x32 = 1.20 * (1.49/1.48) = 1.208` (current) to something
#   like 1.30 or 1.40 — explicitly making DCT32X16 MORE expensive while
#   keeping DCT32X32 at 1.20. Expected to close ~2200/cell on 1531677 and
#   ~450/cell on 1420710. Risk: would over-suppress legitimate DCT32X16
#   picks on partially-textured content.
#
#   Recommended bisection range: 1.30, 1.40, 1.49 (libjxl-parity).
#   Time: 1-2 days (sweep + bisection + multi-decoder roundtrip).
#   EV: 5-7 of 7 cells close. Risk: 1-2 photo SSIM2 cells regress.
#
# Candidate B: Per-distance W44-29 widening — apply W44-96 variant Z lift
#              ALSO at d<4.5 where it currently rejects (d=4 cells).
#              — LOW-MEDIUM RISK
#
#   Mechanism: lower `W44_96_VARIANT_Z_MIN_DISTANCE` (4.5) to 4.0. The 7
#   remaining OPEN cells are all d=5 or d=6, so this doesn't directly
#   close any new cell — but it widens the optimization scope. Test
#   first to see if d=4 1420710/1531677 cells now win.
#
#   EV: 0-1 of 7 OPEN cells close (most are already at d>=5). Useful for
#   broader optimization but won't close W44-97 targets specifically.
#   Skip unless paired with Candidate A.
#
# Candidate C: Per-block QF refinement on cells where DCT32X16 was picked
#              — re-evaluate DCT32X32 with REDUCED qac (matching cjxl's
#              quant field for these blocks) and accept if it now beats
#              the split. — MEDIUM-HIGH RISK
#
#   Mechanism: post-strategy-selection pass that finds DCT32X16 picks
#   adjacent to other DCT32X16 picks (forming a virtual DCT32X32 region)
#   and re-checks whether the merged region's quant field allows DCT32X32
#   to be cheaper.
#
#   Risk: substantial new code path, may interact with AdjustQuantField
#   and butteraugli loop. Estimate ~300 LOC, multi-week implementation.
#   EV: 3-5 cells close. Hash-lock impact: significant.
#
# Candidate D: libjxl source-diff for unported d=5-specific gates
#              (more careful audit than W44-97 quick scan) — INVESTIGATE
#
#   Mechanism: Read libjxl enc_ac_strategy.cc + enc_adaptive_quantization.cc
#   line-by-line and compare against our port for any conditional that
#   would fire at d=5 but not d=4. The W44-97 quick scan found
#   kFavor2X2 (d<5) and kAvoidEntropyOfTransforms (d>4) — both ported —
#   but a more careful 200-line audit may uncover a subtle gate.
#
#   Time: 4-6h. EV: low-medium (most known gates already audited per
#   W44-91 / W44-79 audit history). Risk: low (audit is read-only).
#
# Candidate E: Investigate the "loss component dominates at lower entropy_mul"
#              hypothesis (RC1). Build a per-block decomposition of
#              `entropy * entropy_mul + loss` for DCT32X32 vs 2× DCT32X16
#              on a few hotspot cells. If the loss component IS the
#              tilting factor, refine the W44-29 sub-discriminator to
#              admit DCT32X32 when the loss is the differentiator.
#              — MEDIUM RISK (research, then targeted fix)
#
#   Time: 1-2 days (instrumentation + analysis + tightening).
#   EV: 3-5 cells close, builds analytical foundation for future W44 chunks.
#
# My ranking for W44-98:
#
#   1. **Candidate A** (per-strategy dct16x32 lift in variant Z) — best
#      EV/effort ratio. Closes the dominant DCT32X16 overshoot directly.
#      Bisection-friendly. Low risk because variant Z is gated to 2 images.
#
#   2. **Candidate D** (deeper source-diff audit) — low risk, builds
#      foundation. Should be done in parallel with A as background.
#
#   3. **Candidate E** (loss-component decomposition) — most rigorous,
#      best long-term value. Use if A's bisection doesn't find a clean
#      single value.
#
#   4. **Candidate B** (distance widening) — only as paired booster.
#
#   5. **Candidate C** (re-merge pass) — defer; too expensive.
#
# DO NOT (for future W44-9N agents)
# ==================================
#
# - DO NOT respawn try_dct64 effort gate widening (W44-93 RULED OUT,
#   regressed photo SSIM2 by -1.21 at e5).
# - DO NOT respawn find_best_32x32 entropy_mul tightening as a global
#   change (W44-94 RULED OUT — not enough EV on these 7 cells).
# - DO NOT respawn variant Z dct32x32 lift below 1.20 (W44-95 RULED OUT
#   — wider mask<50 population fails the ≤0.3 SSIM2 budget).
# - DO NOT touch `tokenize_ac_coefficients`, `nz_context`, `zd_offset`
#   — bit-exact per W44-75 audit.
# - DO NOT touch BlockCtxMap / cluster_histograms — W44-71/75 verified.
# - DO NOT touch AdjustQuantBlockAC — W44-76 verified same-strategy
#   nzeros parity.
# - DO NOT cite "FMA precision" as the residual mechanism (per W44-66
#   user correction, `fd_residual_not_fma_precision_2026-05-19.md`).
# - DO NOT respawn cluster_histograms / FastClusterHistograms / HistogramReindex
#   / ZeroDensityContext audits — all at libjxl parity per W44-75.
